WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.909 --> 00:00:05.879
The finale of The Mandalorian's inaugural
season proved to be a massive Pandora's Box

00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:10.820
of lore, paving the way for all the future
conflict to define Mando's return in the second

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:11.820
season.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.940
Let's take a look at how much bigger Mando's
story has become and what it will mean for

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.090
the next hotly-anticipated season, whenever
it arrives.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:25.660
Within the action-packed shell of The Mandalorian
chapter 8, a rich, succulent nugget of characterization

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.039
for Mando was slowly revealed.

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:33.180
His brush with death gave us the first opportunity
we've ever had to actually see Pedro Pascal

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:38.239
in his role as the Mandalorian, when IG-11
removes his helmet to save his life under

00:00:38.239 --> 00:00:43.039
the amusingly convenient technicality that
the droid isn't a living being which means

00:00:43.039 --> 00:00:47.160
Mando wouldn't wound his honor by revealing
his face to him.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.890
"So, what happens if you take that thing off?

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:53.170
They come after you and kill you?"

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:57.070
"No. you just can't ever put it back on again."

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:02.879
It's a deeply humanizing moment for our hero
whose full name has now been spoken on-screen

00:01:02.879 --> 00:01:07.970
to show his face in his most desperate moment,
visibly frightened and lost like any other

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:09.770
person in the face of death.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:15.220
Additionally, it's particularly special because
IG-11 came to represent a mirror for Mando

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.340
and the kind of man he used to be.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.090
After a little help shaking off the grim reaper,
Mando had bestowed upon him an official signet

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:26.920
to represent his complete identity within
the Mandalorian creed, and finally got a slick

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.120
jetpack of his very own.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:34.480
Being of no traditional Mandalorian clan himself,
he has become his own collective unit with

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:40.200
the Child a.k.a. Baby Yoda as a Clan of Two,
and was given his mission: return the Child

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.570
to where it belongs.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:46.791
That said, it's become painfully clear that,
if the Child belongs anywhere, it's with Mando

00:01:46.791 --> 00:01:51.890
but again, that's the overarching emotional
journey we're going on with our favorite chrome-domed

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:55.620
hero as he continues fully realizing that.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:00.010
Underneath his Mandalorian armor, the character
fans have fallen in love with over the course

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:03.970
of the series' first season is named Din Djarin.

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:08.080
Mando's canonical name snuck out into the
public eye some weeks ago, so you may have

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.130
already heard it if you were paying attention,
but at the end of the day, his true moniker

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:16.160
isn't of any terrible consequence or meant
to be a forbidden secret.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:21.640
The importance in revealing his name now on
The Mandalorian chapter 8 is both to underline

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:27.820
just how dangerous Moff Gideon is, and also
to compound the humanization of Mando's character

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.950
that occurs within the episode.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:34.090
Nobody hanging out with him on-screen needs
to call him by his name, but we should hear

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:39.750
it in this particular episode's context, after
we've learned many of this man's deepest truths

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.420
and see him at his lowest.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:46.110
Moff Gideon likely knows very little about
Mando as a person and only knows his name

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:51.650
because it's on an official governmental list
on Mandalore, but Moff and everything he represents

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:55.690
makes for the perfect kind of pre-packaged
Instant Villain for our helmeted hero.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:03:02.460
"You may think you have some idea what you
are in possession of, but you do not."

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:08.310
Moff is directly representative of the Mandalorians'
galactic genocide and the society that robbed

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:13.340
pre-Mandalorian Din Djarin of his original
family, which we have finally gotten to witness

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:17.540
in its totality, and that's before we even
get into the threat the governor represents

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:18.610
to the Child.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:24.820
Truly, Moff Gideon is the spectre shadowing
Mando's entire existence as a person consistently

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.420
robbed of a permanent home and family.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.970
It's perfect narrative poetry: this is the
thing and person from which Mando must defend

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:36.860
his new, tiny clan and we'd say he's doing
a pretty good job of pulling it off.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:41.780
On the season 1 finale of The Mandalorian,
Moff Gideon emerges from his wrecked TIE fighter

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:46.290
and reveals the real twist by holding the
mythical Darksaber.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:49.060
That sure was a last-second lore drop, wasn't
it?

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:54.470
For those unfamiliar, the Darksaber is an
ancient lightsaber dating back over a millennium.

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:58.720
The handmade weapon of the first Mandalorian
inducted into the Jedi Order, the Darksaber

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.830
was stolen in the midst of the Old Republic's
slow, war-ridden demise and then returned

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:05.430
to Mandalore.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:09.580
It stayed there for centuries to become a
generational token passed down to descendants

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:12.569
of the Viszla clan that once ruled the home
planet.

00:04:12.569 --> 00:04:17.329
Very, very long story short: When the Empire
fell, the Darksaber was in the possession

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:23.260
of a Mandalorian woman named Bo-Katan, leader
of the pro-Republic Mandalorian forces.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:27.990
However, that's where the trail ends just
before the Battle of Yavin and the official

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:33.250
fall of the Empire, somewhere between eight
and 10 years prior to this episode of The

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:34.250
Mandalorian.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:38.260
Bo-Katan obviously no longer possesses the
Darksaber, and we can only assume she would

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.820
give it up over her dead body.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:42.860
Ominous, huh?

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:47.990
It's a curious turn for the Star Wars franchise
to pass the lore baton from an animated series

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:54.230
back to a live-action one in such a big way,
and onto such an utterly blank page, too.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:58.760
This is doubly true when you consider that
animated series like The Clone Wars and Rebels

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:02.970
were primarily aimed at children and The Mandalorian
really isn't.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:06.740
When the Empire fell, Mandalore was in a state
of civil war.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:11.200
No further canonical information exists, so
The Mandalorian is gleefully seeking to fill

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.680
those pages up on its own terms.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.960
This is no small side story anymore; the show
will enter a new phase in its second season

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.930
as a full-throated, major piece of canon thanks
to the Darksaber reveal.

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:29.490
If Bo-Katan is indeed dead and the Darksaber
has been stolen from its people, what's the

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:32.370
political state of Mandalore, post-Empire?

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:36.250
The Republic-led forces seemed to be on the
ascent along with the rest of the Rebellion,

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:41.000
but the heart of the conflict for the Mandalorians
in the past several canonical decades was

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:44.820
whether or not to become more pacifist and
relinquish martial culture.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:48.080
How much does that matter to the plot of The
Mandalorian?

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:52.810
And how in blazes did an Imperial Remnant
governor get ahold of the MacGuffin that the

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:54.320
Darksaber represents?

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.840
We'll be sitting on our hands for the next
several months waiting to find out when the

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.840
second season of The Mandalorian arrives,
hopefully sometime in 2020.

